# Onboarding, integration setup and sign-in

Explain what is being set up, what information is required and when a discovered device becomes usable. Preserve Home Assistant’s native ownership and authentication flow. The installed civica companion does not replace onboarding, sign-in, multi-factor authentication or native app identity.

## Scope and native ownership

Native onboarding offers creating a new installation or restoring a backup and then establishing its configuration. Integration setup and reauthentication belong to their native configuration flows. These examples are guidance specimens, not a credential collector. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/getting-started/onboarding/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-021**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| New installation / restore | Explain the distinct starting paths and their consequences. | Do not imply creating an owner restores an existing home. |
| Discovered | Name the candidate device/service and the next supported setup step. | Discovered does not mean configured or connected. |
| Configuring / awaiting provider | Retain the chosen integration and real flow state. | Do not fabricate completion while waiting for authorization. |
| Configured, no entities yet | Explain the actual reported condition and available diagnostics. | Do not silently treat an empty entity list as failure or success. |
| Reauthentication required | Identify the provider and affected integration. | Never ask for a password in a generic themed alert. |

## Integration contract

1. Keep owner creation, login credentials, people records and device discovery separate. Appearance cannot grant administrative access.
2. Use the native integration schema and provider redirect. Explain actual requested permissions and account scope before proceeding; do not promise local processing for an external provider.
3. Preserve the selected device, non-secret setup answers and native validation where supported. Do not copy credentials into URLs, example source, logs or analytics.
4. After configuration, distinguish the integration entry being saved from devices/entities reporting useful state. An authentication success alone does not prove device readiness.

## Accessible interaction

- Use visible labels and programmatic hints/errors; do not disable password managers or paste in actual native credential fields.
- Support a clear return path after an external authorization step and preserve keyboard focus on the native error or next task.
- Keep location, analytics and optional discovery choices explicit. Explain why sensitive data are requested rather than making all choices appear required.

## Failure and recovery

- When discovery shows duplicate candidates, use verified identifiers to distinguish them without displaying unnecessary secrets.
- For rejected authorization, preserve the native error and account scope; offer the actual retry or cancel route.
- Cancelling setup must describe whether a partial integration entry remains. Do not invent removal of an already configured integration.

## Complete examples

The examples use invented records. Forms validate and show local feedback only; they do not call Home Assistant, save preferences or claim a real device result. In-page links lead to the explanation shown in the same specimen.

